============================================================
PART 1: UNDERSTANDING ROUTES
============================================================

1. WHAT IS A ROUTE?

A route tells Laravel:

- Which URL a user can access.
- Which HTTP method is accepted.
- Which controller method or function should execute.
- Which middleware should process the request.
- What response should be returned.

Example:

Route::get('/employees', function () {
    return 'Employee List';
});

Explanation:

Route:
Laravel's routing facade.

get():
Registers a route that responds to HTTP GET requests.

'/employees':
The URL path.

function():
The closure that Laravel executes when the route matches.

Example URL:

http://localhost:8000/employees

When this URL receives a GET request, Laravel returns:

Employee List


============================================================
2. WHERE ARE ROUTES REGISTERED?
============================================================

Laravel provides different route files for different purposes.

routes/web.php
    Used for browser-based web pages, sessions, cookies, and form submissions.

routes/api.php
    Used for API endpoints, typically stateless and prefixed with /api.

routes/console.php
    Used for console commands and scheduling-related definitions.

routes/channels.php
    Used for broadcasting channel authorization when broadcasting is configured.

IMPORTANT:

Depending on how your Laravel application was created, routes/api.php may not exist initially.

In Laravel 11 and 12, you can configure API routing using:

php artisan install:api

This installs the API routing setup and Laravel Sanctum as part of the standard setup process.

Review the command's effects before running it in an existing application.

For the Leave Management System:

Web routes:
routes/web.php

API routes:
routes/api.php

Web controllers:
app/Http/Controllers/

API controllers:
app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/

Reusable business logic:
app/Services/


============================================================
PART 2: BASIC HTTP ROUTES
============================================================

3. GET ROUTE

Purpose:
Retrieve or display information.

Syntax:

Route::get('/employees', function () {
    return 'Display Employees';
});

Controller version:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
]);

Typical uses:
- Display an employee list.
- Display employee details.
- Display a dashboard.
- Retrieve API data.


4. POST ROUTE

Purpose:
Submit data, commonly to create a resource or perform an action.

Syntax:

Route::post('/employees', function () {
    return 'Create Employee';
});

Controller version:

Route::post('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'store',
]);

Typical use:
Submit an employee creation form.


5. PUT ROUTE

Purpose:
Replace a resource's representation, generally by submitting the complete set of fields that should be updated.

Syntax:

Route::put('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'update',
]);


6. PATCH ROUTE

Purpose:
Partially update a resource.

Syntax:

Route::patch('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'update',
]);

Example:
Update only an employee's salary without submitting every employee field.

PUT and PATCH often use the same Laravel controller method, but their intended HTTP semantics differ.


7. DELETE ROUTE

Purpose:
Request deletion of a resource.

Syntax:

Route::delete('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'destroy',
]);


8. OPTIONS ROUTE

Purpose:
Respond to requests asking which communication options are available for a resource.

Example:

Route::options('/employees', function () {
    return response('', 204)
        ->header('Allow', 'GET, POST, OPTIONS');
});

This is an illustrative response. Real applications may need to account for supported methods and CORS configuration.


9. MATCH MULTIPLE HTTP METHODS

Syntax:

Route::match(
    ['get', 'post'],
    '/employee-search',
    function () {
        return 'Employee Search';
    }
);

This route accepts both GET and POST requests.


10. ACCEPT ANY HTTP METHOD

Syntax:

Route::any('/webhook', function () {
    return 'Webhook Endpoint';
});

This registers a route for all HTTP methods supported by Laravel's any() registration.

Avoid using any() for sensitive operations unless you have a specific reason.

Explicit HTTP methods make the application's behavior easier to understand and protect.


============================================================
PART 3: ROUTES CONNECTED TO CONTROLLERS
============================================================

11. ROUTE TO A CONTROLLER METHOD

Syntax:

Route::get(
    '/employees',
    [EmployeeController::class, 'index']
);

Laravel calls:

EmployeeController::index()

General syntax:

Route::HTTP_METHOD(
    '/url',
    [ControllerClass::class, 'methodName']
);

Example CRUD routes:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
]);

Route::get('/employees/create', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'create',
]);

Route::post('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'store',
]);

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
]);

Route::get('/employees/{employee}/edit', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'edit',
]);

Route::put('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'update',
]);

Route::delete('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'destroy',
]);


12. SINGLE-ACTION CONTROLLER

Create:

php artisan make:controller DashboardController --invokable

Controller:

<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use Illuminate\View\View;

class DashboardController extends Controller
{
    public function __invoke(): View
    {
        return view('dashboard');
    }
}

Route:

Route::get('/dashboard', DashboardController::class);

Laravel calls the controller's __invoke() method.

Use this when a controller has one clear responsibility.


============================================================
PART 4: RESOURCE ROUTES
============================================================

13. REGISTER ALL RESOURCE ROUTES

Syntax:

Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class);

Laravel registers the conventional CRUD routes.

HTTP METHOD    URL                              METHOD       ROUTE NAME

GET            /employees                       index        employees.index
GET            /employees/create                create       employees.create
POST           /employees                       store        employees.store
GET            /employees/{employee}             show         employees.show
GET            /employees/{employee}/edit        edit         employees.edit
PUT/PATCH      /employees/{employee}             update       employees.update
DELETE         /employees/{employee}             destroy      employees.destroy

Purpose of each controller method:

index()
    Display a list of employees.

create()
    Display the employee creation form.

store()
    Save a new employee.

show()
    Display one employee.

edit()
    Display the employee editing form.

update()
    Update an employee.

destroy()
    Delete an employee.

Laravel expands the resource declaration into individual registered routes.


14. REGISTER ONLY SELECTED RESOURCE ACTIONS

Syntax:

Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class)
    ->only(['index', 'show']);

Only the index and show routes are registered.


15. EXCLUDE RESOURCE ACTIONS

Syntax:

Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class)
    ->except(['destroy']);

All standard resource routes except deletion are registered.


16. API RESOURCE ROUTES

Syntax:

Route::apiResource('employees', EmployeeApiController::class);

API resource routes include:

index()
store()
show()
update()
destroy()

They exclude create() and edit() because API endpoints generally do not need HTML form-display routes.


17. REGISTER MULTIPLE RESOURCES

Syntax:

Route::resources([
    'employees' => EmployeeController::class,
    'departments' => DepartmentController::class,
    'projects' => ProjectController::class,
]);


18. NESTED RESOURCE ROUTES

Example:

Route::resource(
    'departments.employees',
    DepartmentEmployeeController::class
);

Typical URLs:

GET    /departments/{department}/employees
POST   /departments/{department}/employees
GET    /departments/{department}/employees/{employee}
PUT    /departments/{department}/employees/{employee}
DELETE /departments/{department}/employees/{employee}

Use nested resources when an operation is scoped to a parent resource.

IMPORTANT:

Nested URLs do not automatically guarantee that an employee belongs to the specified department.

Use scoped route-model binding or appropriate application logic and authorization.


============================================================
PART 5: VIEW ROUTES AND REDIRECT ROUTES
============================================================

19. RETURN A VIEW DIRECTLY

Syntax:

Route::view('/about', 'about');

Laravel displays:

resources/views/about.blade.php

Pass data to the view:

Route::view('/welcome', 'welcome', [
    'name' => 'Rahul',
]);

Inside welcome.blade.php:

<h1>Welcome, {{ $name }}</h1>

Use Route::view() when the route only needs to display a simple view.


20. REDIRECT TO ANOTHER URL

Syntax:

Route::redirect('/old-employees', '/employees');


21. REDIRECT TO A NAMED ROUTE

Syntax:

Route::get('/employee-home', function () {
    return redirect()->route('employees.index');
});


22. PERMANENT REDIRECT

Syntax:

Route::redirect('/old-employees', '/employees', 301);

HTTP 301 indicates a permanent redirect.


============================================================
PART 6: NAMED ROUTES
============================================================

23. REGISTER A NAMED ROUTE

Syntax:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->name('employees.index');


24. GENERATE ITS URL

$url = route('employees.index');


25. REDIRECT TO THE NAMED ROUTE

return redirect()->route('employees.index');


26. GENERATE A URL WITH A PARAMETER

Route:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
])->name('employees.show');

Generate URL:

$url = route('employees.show', [
    'employee' => 10,
]);

Resulting path:

/employees/10


27. USE A NAMED ROUTE IN BLADE

<a href="{{ route('employees.index') }}">
    View Employees
</a>

BENEFITS:

- Avoids hardcoding URLs throughout the application.
- Makes redirects easier to maintain.
- Makes Blade links easier to read.
- Allows URL paths to change without changing every reference.


