PART 8: ROUTE GROUPS
============================================================

37. GROUP ROUTES WITH MIDDLEWARE

Syntax:

Route::middleware(['auth'])->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [
        DashboardController::class,
        'index',
    ]);

    Route::get('/employees', [
        EmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

All routes in the group receive the specified middleware.


38. GROUP ROUTES WITH A URL PREFIX

Syntax:

Route::prefix('admin')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [
        AdminDashboardController::class,
        'index',
    ]);

    Route::get('/employees', [
        AdminEmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

Resulting URLs:

/admin/dashboard
/admin/employees


39. GROUP ROUTES WITH A NAME PREFIX

Syntax:

Route::name('admin.')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [
        AdminDashboardController::class,
        'index',
    ])->name('dashboard');

    Route::get('/employees', [
        AdminEmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ])->name('employees');
});

Route names:

admin.dashboard
admin.employees

IMPORTANT:

prefix('admin')
    Changes URL paths.

name('admin.')
    Changes route names.

They are different features.


40. COMBINE PREFIX, NAME, AND MIDDLEWARE

Syntax:

Route::prefix('admin')
    ->name('admin.')
    ->middleware(['auth', 'can:access-admin'])
    ->group(function () {
        Route::get('/dashboard', [
            AdminDashboardController::class,
            'index',
        ])->name('dashboard');

        Route::resource(
            'employees',
            AdminEmployeeController::class
        );
    });

Examples:

URL:
    /admin/dashboard

Route name:
    admin.dashboard

URL:
    /admin/employees

Route name:
    admin.employees.index

The access-admin authorization ability must be configured.


41. GROUP ROUTES BY CONTROLLER

Syntax:

Route::controller(ReportController::class)->group(function () {
    Route::get('/reports', 'index')->name('reports.index');

    Route::get('/reports/monthly', 'monthly')
        ->name('reports.monthly');

    Route::get('/reports/annual', 'annual')
        ->name('reports.annual');
});


42. NESTED ROUTE GROUPS

Syntax:

Route::prefix('admin')
    ->name('admin.')
    ->middleware(['auth'])
    ->group(function () {
        Route::get('/dashboard', [
            AdminDashboardController::class,
            'index',
        ])->name('dashboard');

        Route::prefix('hr')
            ->name('hr.')
            ->group(function () {
                Route::get('/employees', [
                    HrEmployeeController::class,
                    'index',
                ])->name('employees.index');
            });
    });

Nested HR route:

URL:
    /admin/hr/employees

Route name:
    admin.hr.employees.index


43. DOMAIN ROUTES

Syntax:

Route::domain('admin.example.com')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [
        AdminDashboardController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

Resulting URL:

admin.example.com/dashboard

DNS and web server configuration must direct the domain to the Laravel application.


44. SUBDOMAIN PARAMETERS

Syntax:

Route::domain('{tenant}.example.com')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', function (string $tenant) {
        return "Tenant: {$tenant}";
    });
});

The tenant value is taken from the subdomain.

A multi-tenant application must verify that the tenant exists and that the authenticated user is permitted to access it.


============================================================
PART 9: SPECIAL ROUTE REGISTRATIONS
============================================================

45. FALLBACK ROUTE

Syntax:

Route::fallback(function () {
    return response()->view('errors.404', [], 404);
});

Create the view:

resources/views/errors/404.blade.php

Purpose:
Display a custom not-found page for requests that do not match another route.

Register fallback routes after ordinary routes.

Laravel also handles other 404 conditions, including model-not-found exceptions.


46. RATE-LIMIT A ROUTE

Syntax:

Route::get('/reports', [
    ReportController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('throttle:60,1');

This applies a limit of 60 requests per minute using the standard throttle syntax.

Actual behavior depends on limiter configuration and how requests are counted.


47. SIGNED URLS

Generate a temporary signed URL:

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL;

$url = URL::temporarySignedRoute(
    'invoices.download',
    now()->addMinutes(30),
    ['invoice' => $invoice->id]
);

Register the route:

Route::get('/invoices/{invoice}/download', [
    InvoiceController::class,
    'download',
])
    ->middleware('signed')
    ->name('invoices.download');

Laravel checks the signature and expiration.

IMPORTANT:

A valid signed URL does not automatically authorize access to a private resource.

Use authorization checks when required.


48. SCOPED ROUTE-MODEL BINDING

Example:

Route::get(
    '/departments/{department}/employees/{employee}',
    [DepartmentEmployeeController::class, 'show']
)->scopeBindings();

Controller:

public function show(
    Department $department,
    Employee $employee
) {
    return view('employees.show', compact('department', 'employee'));
}

With correctly defined Eloquent relationships, scoped binding can resolve the employee through the department's employees relationship.

This helps prevent a request from combining a department ID with an employee belonging to another department.


