============================================================
PART 10: UNDERSTANDING MIDDLEWARE
============================================================

50. WHAT IS MIDDLEWARE?

Middleware is code that processes an HTTP request before it reaches its destination and can process the response as it travels back.

Think of middleware as a security checkpoint.

For example, when a user accesses:

/employees

The application may check:

1. Is the user authenticated?
2. Is the user authorized?
3. Is the user verified?
4. Has the user exceeded a rate limit?
5. Is the request otherwise acceptable?

Middleware can perform these checks before the controller executes.

Example:

Route::get('/dashboard', [
    DashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth');

If authentication succeeds, the request can proceed, provided other checks succeed.

If authentication fails, Laravel normally redirects browser requests to a login page or returns an unauthenticated response for an API request, depending on the application's configuration and request handling.


51. MIDDLEWARE VERSUS CONTROLLER

MIDDLEWARE:
- Processes requests and responses around the destination.
- Commonly checks authentication and request conditions.
- Can reject a request before the controller executes.
- Can inspect or modify the outgoing response.
- Can be shared across many routes.

CONTROLLER:
- Handles the matched route's action.
- Commonly validates input and coordinates application operations.
- Calls models and services.
- Chooses or returns a response.

Example:

Middleware:
    Check whether the user is authenticated.

Controller:
    Retrieve the employee list.


============================================================
PART 11: TYPES OF MIDDLEWARE
============================================================

52. GLOBAL MIDDLEWARE

Global middleware applies to requests passing through the global HTTP middleware pipeline.

In modern Laravel applications, middleware configuration is commonly found in:

bootstrap/app.php

Example configuration:

use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    // Configure middleware here.
})

Create middleware:

php artisan make:middleware LogRequest

Register it globally:

use App\Http\Middleware\LogRequest;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    $middleware->append(LogRequest::class);
});

append():
    Adds middleware to the end of the global stack.

prepend():
    Adds middleware to the beginning of the global stack.

Example:

$middleware->prepend(LogRequest::class);

Use global middleware when the behavior should apply broadly to HTTP requests.

Middleware ordering matters.


53. ROUTE MIDDLEWARE

Route middleware is attached to an individual route.

Example:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth');

Multiple middleware:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'verified']);

Middleware can also be attached to POST routes:

Route::post('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'store',
])->middleware(['auth', 'can:create-employees']);

The create-employees authorization ability must be defined.


54. MIDDLEWARE GROUPS

A middleware group is a named collection of middleware.

Common Laravel groups:

web:
    Used for typical browser-based web requests.

api:
    Used for API requests.

The exact middleware stack depends on application configuration and Laravel version.


55. THE WEB MIDDLEWARE GROUP

The web group provides middleware for typical browser-based applications.

Its standard responsibilities include:

- Cookie handling.
- Session handling.
- CSRF protection.
- Route-model binding.

Example:

Route::middleware('web')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/employees', [
        EmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

In a standard Laravel application, routes in routes/web.php normally receive the web middleware group automatically.


56. THE API MIDDLEWARE GROUP

The api group is designed for API requests.

Depending on configuration, it may include middleware for API-related behavior such as throttling and route-model binding.

Example:

Route::middleware('api')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/employees', [
        EmployeeApiController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

When routes/api.php is configured normally, Laravel applies the API middleware group and configured API prefix automatically.

You generally do not need to apply the api group manually to every route in routes/api.php.


57. CREATE A CUSTOM MIDDLEWARE GROUP

In Laravel 12, configure a custom group in bootstrap/app.php.

Example:

use App\Http\Middleware\CheckEmployeeStatus;
use App\Http\Middleware\EnsureHrRole;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    $middleware->group('hr', [
        CheckEmployeeStatus::class,
        EnsureHrRole::class,
    ]);
});

Apply it:

Route::middleware('hr')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/hr/employees', [
        HrEmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});

A custom group does not automatically implement authorization. The middleware classes must contain the required logic.


58. MIDDLEWARE ALIASES

An alias is a short name for a middleware class.

Example:

Route::get('/dashboard', [
    DashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth');

Here, auth is a middleware alias.


59. REGISTER A CUSTOM MIDDLEWARE ALIAS

Create middleware:

php artisan make:middleware EnsureHrRole

In bootstrap/app.php:

use App\Http\Middleware\EnsureHrRole;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    $middleware->alias([
        'hr' => EnsureHrRole::class,
    ]);
});

Use it:

Route::get('/hr/dashboard', [
    HrDashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'hr']);


60. REGISTER MULTIPLE ALIASES

$middleware->alias([
    'hr' => EnsureHrRole::class,
    'employee.active' => CheckEmployeeStatus::class,
]);

Use them:

Route::middleware([
    'auth',
    'employee.active',
    'hr',
])->group(function () {
    Route::get('/hr/employees', [
        HrEmployeeController::class,
        'index',
    ]);
});


============================================================
PART 12: MIDDLEWARE WITH PARAMETERS
============================================================

61. ROUTE WITH A MIDDLEWARE PARAMETER

Example:

Route::get('/admin/dashboard', [
    AdminDashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'role:admin']);

Here:

role:
    Middleware alias.

admin:
    Middleware parameter.


62. CREATE ROLE MIDDLEWARE

File:

app/Http/Middleware/CheckRole.php

Example:

<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class CheckRole
{
    public function handle(
        Request $request,
        Closure $next,
        string $role
    ): Response {
        if (
            ! $request->user()
            || $request->user()->role !== $role
        ) {
            abort(403);
        }

        return $next($request);
    }
}

Register the alias in bootstrap/app.php:

use App\Http\Middleware\CheckRole;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    $middleware->alias([
        'role' => CheckRole::class,
    ]);
});

Use it:

Route::get('/admin/dashboard', [
    AdminDashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'role:admin']);

This example assumes the authenticated user has a role attribute.

In applications with multiple roles and permissions, policies, Gates, or a proper permission system may be more appropriate.


63. MIDDLEWARE WITH MULTIPLE PARAMETERS

Route:

Route::get('/reports', [
    ReportController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('role:hr,admin');

Middleware:

public function handle(
    Request $request,
    Closure $next,
    string ...$roles
): Response {
    $user = $request->user();

    if (
        ! $user
        || ! in_array($user->role, $roles, true)
    ) {
        abort(403);
    }

    return $next($request);
}

This example allows users whose role matches one of the supplied values.

Make sure the user model and authorization design match the application's actual requirements.


============================================================
PART 13: CREATE CUSTOM MIDDLEWARE STEP BY STEP
============================================================

64. STEP 1: GENERATE MIDDLEWARE

Command:

php artisan make:middleware CheckEmployeeStatus

Laravel creates:

app/Http/Middleware/CheckEmployeeStatus.php


65. STEP 2: WRITE THE MIDDLEWARE

Example:

<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class CheckEmployeeStatus
{
    public function handle(
        Request $request,
        Closure $next
    ): Response {
        $user = $request->user();

        if (! $user || ! $user->is_active) {
            abort(403, 'Your account is inactive.');
        }

        return $next($request);
    }
}

This example assumes the authenticated user model has an is_active attribute.

If employee status is stored on a separate Employee model, retrieve and check that relationship instead.


66. STEP 3: REGISTER THE ALIAS

In bootstrap/app.php:

use App\Http\Middleware\CheckEmployeeStatus;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Configuration\Middleware;

->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware): void {
    $middleware->alias([
        'employee.active' => CheckEmployeeStatus::class,
    ]);
});

If a withMiddleware() callback already exists, add the alias to the existing callback instead of creating a duplicate callback chain.


67. STEP 4: APPLY THE MIDDLEWARE

Route:

Route::get('/employee/dashboard', [
    EmployeeDashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'employee.active']);


68. STEP 5: TEST THE BEHAVIOR

Test these cases:

1. A guest tries to access the dashboard.
2. An authenticated active employee accesses the dashboard.
3. An authenticated inactive employee accesses the dashboard.

Expected behavior:

Guest:
    Authentication middleware handles the request.

Active employee:
    Can proceed if all other checks pass.

Inactive employee:
    Receives a forbidden response.

IMPORTANT:

The middleware's behavior depends on the actual user and employee data structure.


69. UNDERSTAND $next($request)

Example:

public function handle(
    Request $request,
    Closure $next
): Response {
    // Code before the downstream pipeline.

    $response = $next($request);

    // Code after the downstream pipeline returns.

    return $response;
}

$next($request) tells Laravel to continue processing through the remaining pipeline.

If middleware returns a response before calling $next($request), the downstream middleware and controller do not execute for that request.

Middleware does not have to call $next() in every branch. It can intentionally terminate processing.


============================================================
PART 14: BUILT-IN LARAVEL MIDDLEWARE
============================================================

70. AUTH MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Require the user to be authenticated.

Example:

Route::get('/dashboard', [
    DashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth');

Protect multiple routes:

Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/dashboard', [
        DashboardController::class,
        'index',
    ]);

    Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class);
});

Specify a guard:

Route::get('/admin/dashboard', [
    AdminDashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth:admin');

The admin guard must be configured in config/auth.php.


71. GUEST MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Restrict a route to users who are not authenticated for the applicable guard.

Example:

Route::get('/login', [
    AuthController::class,
    'showLoginForm',
])->middleware('guest');

Difference:

auth:
    User must be authenticated.

guest:
    User must not be authenticated.

The example assumes the controller method exists.


72. VERIFIED MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Require the authenticated user to have a verified email address.

Example:

Route::get('/dashboard', [
    DashboardController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'verified']);

This requires the application's email-verification workflow to be configured.


73. CAN MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Check authorization using an ability.

Example:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}/edit', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'edit',
])->middleware('can:update,employee');

The update ability must be defined through Laravel's authorization system.

Example policy:

public function update(
    User $user,
    Employee $employee
): bool {
    return $user->id === $employee->user_id;
}

This assumes Employee has a user_id identifying its owner.


74. THROTTLE MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Limit request frequency.

Example:

Route::post('/login', [
    AuthController::class,
    'login',
])->middleware('throttle:5,1');

This uses a limit of five requests per minute.

A named rate limiter can also be configured and referenced:

Route::post('/reports', [
    ReportController::class,
    'store',
])->middleware('throttle:reports');

The reports limiter must be registered in the application.


75. PASSWORD.CONFIRM MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Require password confirmation before a sensitive operation.

Example:

Route::delete('/account', [
    AccountController::class,
    'destroy',
])->middleware(['auth', 'password.confirm']);

This depends on the application's password-confirmation workflow.


76. SIGNED MIDDLEWARE

Purpose:
Validate a signed URL.

Example:

Route::get('/invitations/{invitation}', [
    InvitationController::class,
    'show',
])->middleware('signed');

A signed URL should be generated using Laravel's signing mechanisms.

A signature does not automatically establish that the current user has permission to access the resource.


77. CSRF PROTECTION

CSRF means Cross-Site Request Forgery.

It helps prevent unwanted authenticated browser requests.

A Blade form should include:

<form action="{{ route('employees.store') }}" method="POST">
    @csrf

    <input type="text" name="name">

    <button type="submit">Create Employee</button>
</form>

For a PUT request:

<form
    action="{{ route('employees.update', $employee) }}"
    method="POST"
>
    @csrf
    @method('PUT')

    <input
        type="text"
        name="name"
        value="{{ $employee->name }}"
    >

    <button type="submit">Update Employee</button>
</form>

The browser submits POST, while @method('PUT') tells Laravel to interpret it as PUT.

IMPORTANT:

- CSRF protection is different from authentication.
- Browser requests using sessions and cookies have different security considerations from typical stateless API requests using bearer tokens.
- Do not disable CSRF protection merely to fix a form error.


78. ROUTE-MODEL BINDING MIDDLEWARE

Laravel includes SubstituteBindings in the normal routing process.

Example route:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
]);

Controller:

public function show(Employee $employee)
{
    return view('employees.show', compact('employee'));
}

Laravel resolves the employee route parameter into an Employee model.

If the employee cannot be found, Laravel normally returns a 404 response.

Route-model binding does not automatically authorize the user to access the employee.


79. OTHER USEFUL MIDDLEWARE AND FEATURES

auth:
    Require authentication.

guest:
    Restrict access to guests.

verified:
    Require email verification.

can:
    Check an authorization ability.

throttle:
    Limit request frequency.

signed:
    Validate signed URLs.

password.confirm:
    Require password confirmation.

SubstituteBindings:
    Resolve route-model binding.

web:
    Web-oriented middleware group.

api:
    API-oriented middleware group.

Custom middleware:
    Application-specific request checks.

Other middleware concerns can include:

- Cookie encryption.
- Session handling.
- String normalization.
- Request logging.
- Security headers.
- Trusted proxies.
- CORS.
- HTTPS behavior.

The exact middleware classes and ordering depend on the Laravel version and application configuration.


============================================================
PART 15: HOW LARAVEL PROCESSES A REQUEST
============================================================

80. COMPLETE REQUEST LIFECYCLE

Example route:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware(['auth', 'verified']);

Request:

GET /employees

Simplified lifecycle:

1. Browser sends an HTTP request.
2. Web server forwards it to Laravel.
3. Laravel bootstraps the application.
4. Global middleware executes.
5. Laravel matches the URL and HTTP method.
6. Applicable route middleware executes.
7. Route-model binding and other route processing occur.
8. Controller method executes.
9. Models and services perform application operations.
10. Database operations occur as required.
11. Controller returns a response.
12. Middleware may process the response on the way back.
13. Laravel sends the response to the browser.

This is a simplified conceptual sequence. The exact ordering depends on application configuration and the middleware stack.


81. STEP 1: BROWSER SENDS A REQUEST

Example:

GET /employees


82. STEP 2: WEB SERVER FORWARDS THE REQUEST

A web server such as Apache or Nginx forwards appropriate requests to Laravel's front controller:

public/index.php


83. STEP 3: LARAVEL BOOTSTRAPS THE APPLICATION

Laravel loads the application and prepares its services and HTTP request handling.


84. STEP 4: GLOBAL MIDDLEWARE EXECUTES

Global middleware can inspect or process the request.

Example:
A logging middleware may record information about the request.


85. STEP 5: LARAVEL MATCHES THE ROUTE

Laravel matches:

GET /employees

To:

EmployeeController::index()


86. STEP 6: ROUTE MIDDLEWARE EXECUTES

The route requires:

['auth', 'verified']

Authentication middleware checks the user's authentication state.

Email-verification middleware checks whether the user meets the verification requirement.

If a check fails, the request may be redirected or rejected before the controller executes normally.


87. STEP 7: ROUTE-MODEL BINDING

If the route contains model parameters, Laravel resolves them through route-model binding.

The example route has no model parameter to resolve.


88. STEP 8: CONTROLLER EXECUTES

Example:

public function index()
{
    $employees = Employee::paginate(10);

    return view('employees.index', compact('employees'));
}


89. STEP 9: DATABASE IS QUERIED

Eloquent retrieves the employees required for the current page.


90. STEP 10: RESPONSE IS CREATED

Laravel renders:

resources/views/employees/index.blade.php

The resulting HTML becomes the response.


91. STEP 11: MIDDLEWARE PROCESSES THE RESPONSE

Middleware that wraps the downstream request can inspect or modify the response as it travels back through the pipeline.


92. STEP 12: BROWSER RECEIVES THE RESPONSE

The browser displays the employee page.

KEY IDEA:

Middleware does not replace routes or controllers.

It forms part of the pipeline through which requests and responses travel.


============================================================
PART 16: MIDDLEWARE EXECUTION ORDER
============================================================

93. UNDERSTAND MIDDLEWARE ORDER

Example:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware([
    'auth',
    'verified',
    'log.flow',
]);

Conceptual flow:

Request
    |
    v
auth: before
    |
    v
verified: before
    |
    v
log.flow: before
    |
    v
Controller
    |
    v
log.flow: after
    |
    v
verified: after
    |
    v
auth: after
    |
    v
Response

This illustrates the wrapping behavior of middleware that calls $next($request) and continues processing the response afterward.

IMPORTANT:

Do not assume that written order is always the final execution order in every configuration.

Laravel's middleware priority configuration and other framework processing can affect effective ordering.

Order matters when middleware depends on authentication, sessions, or resolved route parameters.


94. LOGGING BEFORE AND AFTER THE DOWNSTREAM PIPELINE

Create middleware:

php artisan make:middleware LogRequestFlow

Implementation:

<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class LogRequestFlow
{
    public function handle(
        Request $request,
        Closure $next
    ): Response {
        Log::info('Before the controller');

        $response = $next($request);

        Log::info('After the controller');

        return $response;
    }
}

Register the alias:

$middleware->alias([
    'log.flow' => LogRequestFlow::class,
]);

Apply it:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('log.flow');

Expected log messages when the middleware completes normally:

Before the controller
After the controller

IMPORTANT:

The second message does not necessarily mean the controller executed.

A downstream middleware might have returned a response before the controller was reached.


============================================================
PART 17: TERMINABLE MIDDLEWARE
============================================================

95. WHAT IS TERMINABLE MIDDLEWARE?

A terminable middleware class has a terminate() method.

Laravel can call it after the response has been sent to the client, provided the application lifecycle and server environment support the normal termination process.

Create middleware:

php artisan make:middleware LogAfterResponse

Example:

<?php

namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class LogAfterResponse
{
    public function handle(
        Request $request,
        \Closure $next
    ): Response {
        return $next($request);
    }

    public function terminate(
        Request $request,
        Response $response
    ): void {
        Log::info('Request completed', [
            'url' => $request->fullUrl(),
            'status' => $response->getStatusCode(),
        ]);
    }
}

Register and attach the middleware in the usual way.

Possible uses:

- Request-completion logging.
- Recording response metadata.
- Suitable post-response operations.

IMPORTANT:

Terminable middleware is not a replacement for Laravel queues.

For reliable asynchronous processing, retries, failure handling, or long-running work, a queued job is generally more appropriate.


============================================================
PART 18: EXCLUDING MIDDLEWARE
============================================================

96. EXCLUDE MIDDLEWARE FROM ROUTES

Laravel provides withoutMiddleware() for excluding applicable route middleware.

Example:

Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class)
    ->middleware('auth')
    ->withoutMiddleware(['auth']);

This excludes auth from the generated resource routes.

This example is only for demonstrating the syntax. It is not appropriate if all employee routes must require authentication.

If only some actions need different protection, consider registering the routes separately.

Example:

Route::get('/employees', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'index',
])->middleware('auth');

Route::get('/public-employees', [
    PublicEmployeeController::class,
    'index',
]);

IMPORTANT:

Excluding route middleware does not automatically remove global middleware or every other applicable middleware layer.


============================================================
PART 19: ROUTE ORDER AND COMMON MISTAKES
============================================================

97. SPECIFIC ROUTES BEFORE CONFLICTING DYNAMIC ROUTES

Potentially confusing registration:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
]);

Route::get('/employees/create', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'create',
]);

The dynamic parameter could match the word create unless the routes or constraints distinguish the paths.

Clearer order:

Route::get('/employees/create', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'create',
]);

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
]);

Alternatively:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [
    EmployeeController::class,
    'show',
])->whereNumber('employee');

Use meaningful parameter constraints and avoid ambiguous route definitions.


98. COMMON ROUTING MISTAKES

Mistake 1:
Using the wrong HTTP method.

Solution:
Ensure the form or client method matches the route registration.

Mistake 2:
Forgetting the CSRF token.

Solution:
Include @csrf in applicable Blade forms.

Mistake 3:
Defining conflicting routes.

Solution:
Inspect php artisan route:list.

Mistake 4:
Using auth without authorization.

Solution:
Use policies, Gates, or another suitable authorization mechanism.

Mistake 5:
Assuming a route parameter proves a record exists.

Solution:
Retrieve the record or use route-model binding.

Mistake 6:
Using an unregistered middleware alias.

Solution:
Register the alias in bootstrap/app.php.

Mistake 7:
Confusing URL prefixes with route-name prefixes.

Solution:
Remember that prefix() changes URL paths and name() changes route names.

Mistake 8:
Assuming web and API routes have identical behavior.

Solution:
Use appropriate authentication, CSRF expectations, and response formats for each type of client.


